WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:03.350
- "Grey's Anatomy," season
2, episode 16 and 17,

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.816
I heard were the bomb, so
I'm gonna check it out.

00:00:05.816 --> 00:00:06.904
Beep-boop.

00:00:06.904 --> 00:00:09.388
(sirens blaring)

00:00:09.388 --> 00:00:10.870
(woman screaming)

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.070
Oh.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.980
Okay, immediately, a lot
of blood, screaming person,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.093
paramedics, EMTs need to speak up here.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.680
Um, can't tell what's happening here.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.900
It looks like her arm is inside his chest?

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.210
Is she holding together a broken artery?

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.670
A broken blood vessel
that is bleeding out?

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.570
- Paramedics found him
unconscious and bleeding.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.110
Mechanisms of injury are unknown,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.060
but he has a large sucking chest wound.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.990
- Vitals?
- Tachycardic, in the 140s.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.150
BP holding in the 90s.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.720
- With that massive blood loss,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.920
I expected tachycardia to be worse,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.700
because what happens
is when you lose blood,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.800
your body actually loses blood pressure,

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.560
because there's not enough
liquid inside your blood vessels.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:49.870
When your body drops blood pressure,

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.110
there isn't enough blood
reaching vital areas:

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.590
your brain, your kidneys, your liver.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.480
So what your body does
to compensate for that

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.110
is it tries to pump faster.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.750
That's why the tachycardia happens.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.270
- How's his respiratory effort?

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.060
- Absent breath sounds on the right side.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.130
- Absent breath sounds
on the right sounds like

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.910
that there's a collapsed
lung on that side,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.790
which can happen with
a penetrating injury,

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:08.810
chest wall injury.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.743
- Bubbling at the site of the wound.

00:01:09.743 --> 00:01:11.820
He's shockey, and getting
a little cyanotic.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.570
- Shockey means that his
blood pressure's getting

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.200
quite low, which means that he's not able

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.520
to perfuse his organs.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.860
Cyanotic means that he's
not perfusing his skin well,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.500
meaning that he starts
actually turning blue,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.110
'cause there's not enough
oxygen reaching his skin cells

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:25.610
from the small little capillaries,

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.770
the small little blood vessels

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.510
that feed the skin its oxygenation.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.460
- Why do you have your
hand inside of my patient?

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.920
- I tried to tamponade the wound,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:33.813
with gauze and pressure,
but the only thing

00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:36.420
that would stop the bleeding was my hand.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.510
Every time I try to move
it, he starts bleeding out.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:39.820
- Putting pressure on a wound

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.510
is the most important thing you can do

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.850
other than creating a makeshift
tourniquet on the scene,

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:44.810
because putting pressure

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.600
can actually stop blood flow to that area.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.290
If you stop blood flow, you
can decrease blood loss.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.610
There is a chance that some blood vessels,

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.010
specifically arteries
that are muscular wall,

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:57.260
can actually spasm so hard
that they cut off circulation,

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.880
decreasing blood loss.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.010
- Can I take my hand out now?

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:01.333
- [EMT] You don't stick your
hand inside of a patient

00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:02.790
when you don't know how he was injured.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.160
- Yeah, I know that now.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:04.993
- Out of my room.

00:02:04.993 --> 00:02:05.826
- She gets to stay?

00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:07.710
- She has her hand
stuck inside my patient.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.950
- Yeah, so there should be an
OR being prepped right away.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.970
If you have an EMT telling you that,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.480
if I let go, bleeding is
instantly gonna happen,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.990
that means you need to open up the patient

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:16.900
and prepare them for surgery,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.870
'cause that means there's serious damage

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.840
to one of the important
blood vessels in the chest.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.400
- You have your finger on a major bleeder.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.600
Mr. Carlson is running out of time.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.930
The only thing you have that you have won

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.055
is an all-expense paid trip to the OR.

00:02:28.055 --> 00:02:28.888
(bell rings)

00:02:28.888 --> 00:02:29.721
- [Cristina] What do you want me to do?

00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:33.030
- I want you to make
that woman stop screaming

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.970
and tell us what happened.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.070
- And also, make sure that
everyone wears face shields,

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.070
because what he called a major bleeder

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.228
will squirt in everyone's eyes.

00:02:40.228 --> 00:02:42.710
- Mrs. Carlson.
(woman screams)

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.410
Are you injured?
(woman screams)

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.136
Can you hear me?
(woman screams)

00:02:47.136 --> 00:02:50.210
(Alex screams)
(woman stops screaming)

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.500
- If you are in the medical field,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.680
please don't ever do that.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.513
Support.

00:02:53.513 --> 00:02:54.490
Be a human first.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.520
Do not yell back at
someone who's obviously

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.530
in a state of shock.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.350
- Is this okay? Me
being in here like this?

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.300
- Once Dr. Burke scrubs in,
he'll have you remove your hand,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.710
and then you can go.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.610
- This is obviously not okay,

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.230
because when she put her
hand in, it wasn't sterile.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.697
The patient's gonna need major
IV antibiotics after the fact

00:03:12.697 --> 00:03:14.670
but in this case, it's acceptable.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.503
Why?

00:03:15.503 --> 00:03:16.620
Because had she not done that,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.020
the patient would've bled out and died.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.410
The trade off here of
potentially getting infection,

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.150
but to save his life acutely is warranted.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.780
That's why, whenever
people make overwhelming

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.540
or overgeneralized
statements about medicine,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.373
I have to like, point out

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.330
that it's right sometimes,
wrong sometimes.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.390
It depends on the situation
and on the patient.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.020
It's why it's so hard when you guys ask me

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.790
very specific questions
about your own health

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:35.775
for me to answer them.

00:03:35.775 --> 00:03:36.810
- She's fine.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.170
Acute anxiety reaction,
give her two diazepam PO

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:40.003
to calm her down.

00:03:40.003 --> 00:03:41.110
- Is she in shock?

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:42.190
- I'll say.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.180
- Can you tell us exactly what happened?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.250
- Maybe try a different approach, dude.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.290
How 'bout just asking, are you okay?

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.550
- What happened is, my husband
and his moron best friend-

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:51.860
- Will you stop calling me a moron?

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.860
- Moron best friend.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.730
- Separate them.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.940
- Decide to build some kind of big gun.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.070
- We get an exact replica of the finest

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:02.140
Allied anti-tank weapon of World War II.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.470
- And they try to shoot the thing.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.303
- I'm the gunner.

00:04:04.303 --> 00:04:05.360
James is the loader.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.290
Okay, we followed the
specifications exactly.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.123
You should see it.

00:04:08.123 --> 00:04:10.210
It's a 60 millimeters, one
and a half pound rocket.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.750
- Where is Grey's Anatomy's
hospital? Seattle, right?

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.780
Yeah, missiles aren't legal in Seattle.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.080
I don't need to look that one up.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.040
- He shot himself with a bazooka.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.630
- Like I said, morons, the pair of them.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:21.820
- Was there an explosion?

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:22.653
- Huh?

00:04:22.653 --> 00:04:23.560
- Was there an explosion?

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.623
- No. Why?

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:26.933
Oh, crap.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.830
- How did these guys make a real missile

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.670
in their backyard or garage?

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.220
- Hannah?

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.520
- Yes, Dr. Burke?

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.030
- What do you feel inside of Mr. Carlson?

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.570
- Preface that with please do
not move or remove your hand

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.020
before asking what you feel,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.680
'cause the natural instinct of a human,

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.470
when someone asks what do
you feel is to do this.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.440
Do not do that near a
bleeding artery or a bomb.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.680
Fact you learn on this channel.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.620
- Is your hand touching anything hard?

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.563
- Um, I don't know.

00:04:55.563 --> 00:04:57.080
- Don't move your hand.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.300
- A little late, bud.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.070
- Hannah, I don't want you to move.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.360
- Okay.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:02.820
You should know you're
starting to scare me.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.020
- I would've been a little
bit manipulative in this case,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.250
explained that we're very
concerned about the blood vessel,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.010
and I would put the pressure like that,

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.490
because by worrying about her own life,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.250
she could faint, lose control of the hand,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:14.730
and then, boom, literally boom.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:18.725
- Go and tell the charge nurse
that we have a code black,

00:05:18.725 --> 00:05:21.820
and then tell him to call the bomb squad.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.740
- Every hospital has
their own code structure,

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.600
like on the back of my
badge, you could see,

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.500
if it flips over, there's
like the emergency codes,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.630
and what each one means.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.760
And I'm pretty sure each hospital
has their own designation.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.830
There's no universal
one, although code blue

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:35.330
is usually the one for
someones heart stopping.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:39.273
- So I'm touching live,
unexploded ammunition?

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:41.613
- I'm afraid so.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.570
- But he's not hooked up
to the machine anymore?

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.120
- I had Dr. Milton take
him off the ventilator.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.980
The flow of oxygen posed a danger.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.860
- Did they have to immediately evacuate

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:51.870
the entire hospital?

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.040
Because like, there's
oxygen tanks everywhere,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.680
which if there's an explosion,
is a very big problem.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.380
Oxygen itself is not
necessarily flammable.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.020
It's an accelerant for flames.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:02.853
The more you know.

00:06:02.853 --> 00:06:04.460
- They want us to evacuate.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.090
- Evacuate? Did they say why?

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.781
- No, but your pager said code black.

00:06:07.781 --> 00:06:09.680
(dramatic music)

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.240
- We're sure they said that?

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.360
- Yes.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.310
- Could be a drill.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.050
Even if it's not a
drill, I can't evacuate.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.490
I've got an open brain on the table.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.640
If anybody wants to go, they should go.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.410
Anybody wanna evacuate?

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:23.243
Going once.

00:06:23.243 --> 00:06:25.580
- How 'bout tell people
what they're evacuating for.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.000
Like he didn't even give the full story,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:27.940
and he's like, do you wanna leave?

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:28.773
Tell us why.

00:06:28.773 --> 00:06:30.080
What page did you get? What's happening?

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:30.970
Give us more info.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:32.780
- Dr. Shepard, there's an explosive device

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.613
in the OR next door.

00:06:33.613 --> 00:06:34.960
I need you to evacuate now.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.260
- Yeah, and I've got a guy whose brain

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.340
is exposed on this table.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:38.960
I'm not gonna walk away
and leave him to die.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.820
- I mean, you could reposition
him to a safer area,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:46.230
at least not next door
to where the bomb is.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:47.530
- Pink mist.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:48.682
- Excuse me?

00:06:48.682 --> 00:06:51.800
- That's what the bomb squad
calls you when you blow up.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.720
Bomb goes off and anyone in range explodes

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.270
into a billion pieces.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.900
- This anesthesiologist has
not taken a psych course

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.070
in quite some time.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:04.610
He has a very anxious individual
that's keeping her hand

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:08.300
on a bomb, and instead of saying
warm, reassuring statements

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.810
in order to encourage her and
keep her hand on the bomb,

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.570
he's scaring the life of her,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.310
trying to get her to vasovagal
and pass out on the floor.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:16.523
- Take this.
- What?

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.310
- [Dr. Milton] Go ahead.

00:07:20.317 --> 00:07:23.553
Now I want you to
squeeze it in even beats.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:25.583
Not too fast.

00:07:25.583 --> 00:07:26.603
- Is he gonna leave?

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:29.293
Whoa.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.200
- Dr. Milton?

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:33.530
- I've got kids.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.613
- Oh, that's savage.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:37.383
- That's it.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:39.602
- Oh my God.

00:07:39.602 --> 00:07:41.780
- Where's the anesthesiologist?

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.530
- He, um-
♪ I'm falling down ♪

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.363
- He left.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.182
I think I'm gonna take my hand out now.

00:07:48.182 --> 00:07:49.015
- [Dr. Mike] No!

00:07:49.015 --> 00:07:50.840
- That device, it's homemade,
which means it's unstable

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:52.050
and very unreliable.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.900
Could be a dud, but we
have no way of knowing.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.460
Add to that the fact
that you've got a surgeon

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.170
in the OR next door refusing to leave

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.600
and a nervous paramedic's
hand inside a body cavity.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.400
- Why is this guy running
down a list of everything

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.840
that's wrong with this situation

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.490
and not addressing any of it?

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.240
Imagine you went into a doctor's office,

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.400
and doctors just rattled
off every problem you have

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.060
with no plan of what to do next.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.730
Not a very useful doctor's visit.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.630
- I am 22 years old.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:15.820
I should not even be in here.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:17.410
This is some kind of mistake.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:18.243
- She's panicking.

00:08:18.243 --> 00:08:19.076
We need to clear the room.

00:08:19.076 --> 00:08:22.090
- They need to defuse that situation.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:23.557
- Just a little while longer.

00:08:23.557 --> 00:08:25.090
Hannah.
- I have to.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.840
(dramatic music)

00:08:36.677 --> 00:08:38.136
- Did she switch hands with her?

00:08:38.136 --> 00:08:40.886
(dramatic music)

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.480
- Nobody move, stay exactly where you are.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.540
- This is the most useless
bomb squad person ever.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.860
I swear, if I was there,
with zero bomb experience,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.270
I could've handled this
situation better than this dude.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:53.900
The only thing he's good at is dressing up

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.080
as team bomb squad.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.290
Like he's got the boots, he's
got the whole combat look,

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.890
but no knowledge, no
recommendations, nothing.

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:01.420
- Time for you to go.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:02.310
- No, I'm staying.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.490
- There's nothing more you can do here.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.210
We got it covered.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:08.310
Cristina, this is not
another cool surgery.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.010
This ammo can go off at any time

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:11.230
and kill everyone in this room.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.063
Do you get that?

00:09:12.063 --> 00:09:14.520
- If you do not have a
role in this situation,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.830
you should not be in this situation.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.430
That is how I would say it,
and I would say it quite firmly

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:20.710
and if the person didn't listen,

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.160
I would ask the cool bomb
squad man for his handcuffs

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.990
and address the situation myself.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:29.030
- So you have a plan, right? (sniffs)

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.690
You have a way to get
me out of this, right?

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.460
- I think this bomb
squad guy has never seen

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.660
nor learned about any bomb.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.430
I think he got a Halloween
costume, showed up,

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.500
and was like, damn, wish I studied bombs.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.130
- Emotional damage!
(static crackles)

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.000
- In the OR, we put our
patients on general anesthesia.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:47.833
- Yeah?

00:09:47.833 --> 00:09:49.603
- That involves a steady
flow of pure oxygen.

00:09:49.603 --> 00:09:52.170
(tense music)

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.003
- Yeah?

00:09:53.003 --> 00:09:55.440
- Well, can't you turn
off the oxygen in my OR?

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.583
- I can and I have, but this is your OR.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:04.683
This is the OR floor's main oxygen line.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.220
- We have to move.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.053
- Okay.

00:10:08.053 --> 00:10:10.600
- I can't wiggle my fingers,
because we can't shift

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.830
the ammo, and now you wanna roll out

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:13.663
the entire gurney.

00:10:13.663 --> 00:10:16.200
- Well, that's our safest, safest option.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.140
- What is the concern?

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:18.910
They've evacuated the whole hospital.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.540
You have a person who's gonna die,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.930
whether there's oxygen below you or not.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:24.763
What is the concern?

00:10:24.763 --> 00:10:25.810
The damaging of the equipment?

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.170
I think there's bigger worries than that.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:29.470
First of all, the person
is no way gonna make it.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:31.870
Medically, only in a
fictional medical drama

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:32.703
could this happen.

00:10:32.703 --> 00:10:34.830
I'm surprised the paramedics
didn't start chest compressions

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:37.350
and explode the bomb en
route to the hospital.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.810
I feel like the correct move here is,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:43.060
I think ethically, you have to
open up the patient's chest.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.290
He will start bleeding.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.550
You have to get complete
visual of the bomb,

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.260
and only then, when you're comfortable,

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.180
potentially remove it,
which means, likely,

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:52.570
that the patient will die, but
it decreases the likelihood

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:53.800
that the bomb will go off,

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.070
killing everyone else in the room.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.470
But I think only a
bioethicist could answer

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:58.520
this question correctly.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.460
If this was on my boards,
I would probably fail.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:02.760
If this was an SAT medical question,

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:03.780
I would not answer it,

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.040
'cause a wrong answer loses you points.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:07.600
- I'm going to extend the wound.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.310
When I cut, the bleeding
is going to intensify.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:11.890
If we're going to save Mr. Carlson,

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:13.830
we have to pull the ammo out immediately.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.870
- Patient's no longer
tachycardic up there.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.260
95 pulse.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.790
Doesn't make a whole lot of
sense with all that blood loss.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:21.780
- How is the girl with the bomb?

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.250
- It's Meredith.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.020
The girl with the bomb is Meredith.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.246
- That's wifey.

00:11:26.246 --> 00:11:27.140
(heart rate monitor flatlining)

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:28.790
Maybe instead of talking
about your love interest,

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:29.800
you focus on the patient

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.351
who you're operating on their brain.

00:11:31.351 --> 00:11:33.090
(heartfelt orchestral music)

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.476
At least they're doing chest compressions.

00:11:34.476 --> 00:11:37.240
(heartfelt orchestral music)

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:38.360
Maybe lower the patient.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.690
You can't get adequate pressure
doing chest compressions

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:44.203
when you're on the same
height as the patient.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.340
Oop, he tried the cardiac thump.

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:51.763
Oh, obviously it's a
medical drama miracle.

00:11:51.763 --> 00:11:55.513
(heartfelt orchestral music)

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:04.850
Now what?

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.630
Is my man just gonna hold
it and rock it to sleep?

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.180
I feel like he didn't
even think this through.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:10.810
He's like, what do I do with it now?

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:11.993
It would've been great if
he was like, hey, Siri.

00:12:11.993 --> 00:12:13.390
(Siri beeping)

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:18.223
(heartfelt, suspenseful orchestral music)

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:22.600
Where is he going?

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.683
Don't bring it outside to
where there's other people.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.078
And she's chasing him.

00:12:28.078 --> 00:12:31.947
(tense, heartfelt orchestral music)

00:12:31.947 --> 00:12:32.780
(bomb exploding)
(glass shattering)

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:33.613
No!

00:12:35.227 --> 00:12:37.977
(glass shatters)

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.040
I expected this to have a happy ending.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.353
- Is my husband alive?

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:45.733
- Yes.

00:12:45.733 --> 00:12:47.540
- That's gotta be the most
fictional outcome ever.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:50.380
My man was bleeding out
for five hours straight

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:53.390
with two hands inside of his
body, and he's just fine?

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:54.910
Even if he's alive in this moment,

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:58.280
he's in the most critical
state ever: ICU care.

00:12:58.280 --> 00:12:59.113
- Where is she?

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:02.273
- She's right here.

00:13:02.273 --> 00:13:04.263
- Yeah, she just decided
to chase the bomb.

00:13:06.143 --> 00:13:07.730
(Addison sighs)

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.570
That's his ex-wife, right?

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.730
- Current wife.
- Oh, current wife.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:13.583
But he caught the feels.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.510
She should be evaluated,
not just showered.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.700
Like we should give her a full neuro exam.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:21.700
They're like, oh, let's make sure

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.660
she's clean for the next episode.

00:13:23.660 --> 00:13:27.560
That was at the same time
expected and unexpected.

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:28.970
Pretty impressive.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.990
Did you hear about the eyeball
licking craze from Japan?

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:34.300
Click here to learn the
truth about that story,

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:36.330
and as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:38.997
(upbeat music)

